  "articles": [
    {
      "sequence_id": 1,
      "text": "The grammar explains how certain Hindi verbs have causative forms that indicate making someone or something do the action. A list of common transitive verbs is presented, and special attention is given to causatives such as 'बिठाना' and 'गिराना'. For example, ‘इन को यहाँ बिठाइये’ means 'Please seat them here', using the verb बिठाना to mean 'make someone sit down', which is the causative form of बैठना (to sit). Similarly, ‘उस पेड़ को गिराएगा’ means 'He will fell the tree', where गिराना means 'make something fall', the causative of गिरना (to fall). Other causative pairs include: खाना/खिलाना (eat/feed), देखना/दिखाना (see/show), पढ़ना/पढ़ाना (study/teach), उठना/उठाना (rise/raise), निकलना/निकालना (emerge/take out), and समझना/समझाना (understand/explain). The causative form generally adds an ‘-आना’ ending to the root to indicate that the subject causes another to perform the action."
    }
